# Paste the credential the offline issuer logs under `200`. These requests reach
# the app directly on port 8006, so a credential from another issuer is refused
# 401 oauth.invalid_signature. Start the offline issuer with:
#
#   DIAGRID_QUICKSTART_IDENTITY=local APP_PORT=8006 uv run python main.py
#
# See "## Run Offline Without a Catalyst Project" in README.md. The two requests
# that send no credential at all answer 401 in either mode. Left unedited, the
# placeholder below is not a well-formed JWT, so the credential-bearing requests
# answer 401 {"error": "oauth.decode_error"}.
@token = <THE_CREDENTIAL_LOGGED_UNDER_200>

### Who is calling, with no credential: 401 {"error": "oauth.missing_token"}
### The middleware rejects this before the graph, the model, or any app code runs.
GET http://localhost:8006/whoami

### Who is calling, with a credential: 200 and the verified caller
### subject, tenant and issuer_id are read off the credential the app verified,
### never off the request. Against real Catalyst that is a Catalyst-signed
### identity, and the app never sees your identity provider's original token.
GET http://localhost:8006/whoami
X-Diagrid-User-Token: Bearer {{token}}

### Run the agent as that user: 200
### The reply names the caller because the verified subject reached the tool
### through the graph's config, not because the tool read a header.
POST http://localhost:8006/agent/run
X-Diagrid-User-Token: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "task": "What bookings do I have?"
}

### Run the agent with no credential: 401 {"error": "oauth.missing_token"}
### Same rejection on every route. require_auth is app-wide, not per-path.
POST http://localhost:8006/agent/run
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "task": "What bookings do I have?"
}
